// Shared build configuration consumed by iosApp.xcodeproj.
//
// EVERY VALUE THAT VARIES now lives in the GENERATED fragment included below, rendered from the resolved
// deployment by `scripts/resolve-deployment.py` (capability `deployment-configuration`):
//
//   APP_NAME  BUNDLE_ID  TEAM_ID          — Apple identity
//   ASSOCIATED_DOMAIN                      — the device-facing domain, one value shared with the app's
//                                            LINK_ORIGIN, the AASA the backend serves, and the site
//   APS_ENVIRONMENT                        — DERIVED from the one `channel` discriminator
//   UPLOAD_SCHEME  UPLOAD_HOST             — the two parts both Info.plists compose the OS-read
//                                            BackgroundUploadURLBase from (see below)
//
// The fragment carries BUILD SETTINGS, ENTITLEMENT INPUTS and INFO.PLIST SUBSTITUTION INPUTS ONLY. Every
// value the app or the extension READS at runtime — the upload base, the APNs environment, and the
// crash-reporting environment and DSN — lives in the sibling generated `Deployment.plist`, bundled as a
// resource into BOTH targets.
//
// The one exception is a value WE do not read. `assetsd` validates the background-upload registration
// insert against `BackgroundUploadURLBase` in a bundle's OWN `Info.plist`, and can see no resource we
// bundle; without it `setUploadJobExtensionEnabled(true)` fails with a bare `PHPhotosErrorDomain -1` and
// nothing uploads on that tier (measured on device 2026-08-28; capability `ios-photokit-upload`). An
// `Info.plist` substitution can only read a build setting, and a build setting cannot hold a URL — hence
// `UPLOAD_SCHEME` and `UPLOAD_HOST` below: a scheme enum and a bare host, neither able to contain `//`,
// which the two `Info.plist`s compose into `$(UPLOAD_SCHEME)://$(UPLOAD_HOST)/api/v1` where `//` is data.
// That is composition at the destination, NOT the per-line `$()` escape this file used to rely on: there
// is nothing left to escape. `ios.yml` asserts what it composes to equals that bundle's `uploadBase`.
//
// That split is not organisational. `//` opens a comment ANYWHERE on an xcconfig line and the grammar
// offers no escape, so a value carrying a scheme is truncated silently: the crash-reporting DSN became
// `https:`, which is non-empty, so the SDK failed to start while the in-app bug-report dialog kept
// opening and losing every dump — four mute TestFlight builds. A property list escapes. What remains
// here is only values sourced from literals in authored files, which a human reviewed in a pull request;
// the per-line `$()` comment guard this file used to rely on is GONE rather than generalised, because a
// per-site escape covers what someone remembered.
//
// WHY GENERATED RATHER THAN GUARDED. This file used to hold `ASSOCIATED_DOMAIN` and
// `BACKGROUND_UPLOAD_URL_BASE` as literals, and the :test:architecture guard checked only the first —
// the DEVICE-FACING UPLOAD HOST was pinned by nothing at all. `BUNDLE_ID` and `TEAM_ID` likewise
// duplicated the backend's copies unchecked, while composing the App Attest app id and the AASA
// `appIDs`, where drift fails every attestation and stops every universal link matching, silently.
// Guards are opt-in and cover what someone remembered; generation is total.
//
// The include is NOT optional (`#include?`), deliberately: a missing fragment must be a BUILD ERROR, not
// a silent fall-through to stale values. Run the resolver before building —
// `python3 scripts/resolve-deployment.py prod` — which `ios.yml` does before it archives, and which
// `./gradlew` does at configuration time for the framework build.
//
// Retargeting a development build at a different backend is therefore selecting a different DEPLOYMENT
// (`python3 scripts/resolve-deployment.py <name>`), not overriding a host string on the xcodebuild line:
// one resolution feeds BOTH targets — this fragment their build settings, the generated Deployment.plist
// the values they read — so a single selection covers the app and the background-upload extension
// together. Any host reached over the NETWORK must be HTTPS — default ATS
// applies and no NSAllowsLocalNetworking exception ships, so a baked `http://` network host fails
// silently on device. LOOPBACK is the exception, and it is not one we granted: ATS exempts 127.0.0.1
// itself, so plain HTTP works there with no Info.plist key at all. Measured 2026-08-09 from a real app
// bundle on an iOS 26.5 simulator — a Debug build baked with `http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/v1` reached a
// local backend and got a 201, which is how a simulator talks to the `deno task dev:local` rig. (The `/api/v1` prefix is folded into the rendered upload base: every device client
// interpolates `$base/…` from that one value, so uploads, event creation, listings, enrollment, leave,
// directory, device config, notify, push and attest all target the versioned prefix at once. The event
// link's separate LINK_ORIGIN carries no prefix.)
#include "Deployment.xcconfig"

// Public version (CFBundleShortVersionString), inherited by BOTH the app and the background-upload
// extension targets (this is the project-level base config; neither target pins a target-level
// MARKETING_VERSION any more). This value is the version FLOOR, two-part `X.Y`. It is what a plain,
// LOCAL or SIDELOAD build carries — those have no release tag to compute a version from — and the seed a
// main/internal build carries before any release tag exists. ios.yml COMPUTES the version each main build actually carries as
//   MARKETING_VERSION = max(this floor, latest vX.Y tag with its minor incremented by 1)
// (integer minor bump — 0.9→0.10, never a decimal carry to 1.0 — compared as (major,minor) tuples),
// so builds auto-advance off the last release tag. The floor is normally left alone; bump it here (via
// a PR) ONLY to force a MAJOR jump, e.g. 0.1 → 1.0. There is no longer an App Store "trap": main builds
// reach only the internal TestFlight group (no Beta App Review at any version), and the App Store release
// channel (ios-appstore-release / .github/workflows/ios-appstore-promote.yml) PROMOTES an existing build
// and derives the store version FROM it — it never overrides MARKETING_VERSION and never builds.
//
// ⚠️ THE FLOOR MUST STAY AT OR ABOVE `MIN_APP_VERSION` in `api/src/config.ts`. The backend refuses any
// build declaring less than that minimum with a `426`, and the version a dev build declares IS this
// floor. Raise the minimum without raising the floor and every developer, every sideload and every
// simulator is locked out of the backend by a screen telling them to install from the App Store — while
// TestFlight and release builds, which compute a version off the release tag, are entirely fine. That
// asymmetry is why it is invisible until someone tries to work. `api-deploy.yml` asserts the relation
// (it parses this line), so a half-move fails the run rather than the dev loop.
//
// DELIBERATELY NOT in the generated fragment: the floor is RELEASE POLICY, not a deployment fact and not
// a build-channel fact. Every deployment ships the same floor, and moving it into the resolver would put
// release policy behind a generator for no gain.
// ⚠️ Do NOT set the Version field in Xcode's target → General tab: Xcode would write a target-level
// MARKETING_VERSION back into project.pbxproj, shadowing this value again.
MARKETING_VERSION = 0.4

// Entitlements (path relative to SRCROOT = iosApp/): keychain-sharing (event-link), the App
// Group group.app.snapsync (the background-upload extension's shared ledger container), and the
// associated domain for the event link (from the fragment above). Keychain groups need no portal
// registration; the App Group and Associated Domains DO — each must be registered in the Developer
// portal and enabled on the app.snapsync App ID, or signed device/TestFlight builds fail to provision
// (see the ios-background-upload change, task 2.x). The simulator gate ignores signing.
CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS = iosApp/iosApp.entitlements
